## Exercise 1

Write a function that calculates the density function of a Uniform continuous variable on the interval $\left(a,b\right)$. The uniform density function is defined as 

$$
f\left(x\right)=\begin{cases}
    \frac{1}{b-a} & \;\;\;\textrm{if }a\le x\le b\\
    0 & \;\;\;\textrm{otherwise}
    \end{cases}
$$

Your goal for this exercise is to write a function `duniform(x, a, b)` that takes an arbitrary value of `x` and parameters `a` and `b` and returns the appropriate height of the density function ($1/(b-a)$). For various values of `x`, `a`, and `b`, demonstrate that your function returns the correct density value.

### a) 

Write your function without regard for it working with vectors of data. Demonstrate that it works by calling the function three times, once where $x< a$, once where $a < x < b$, and finally once where $b < x$.
 
```{r}
duniform <- function(x, a, b) { 
  if (x >= a & b >= x) {
    return(1/(b-a)) 
  }else{
    return(0) } 
}

duniform(x = 2, a = 3, b = 5) 
duniform(x = 4, a = 3, b = 5) 
duniform(x = 4, a = 3, b = 2) 
```

For subquestion a, I created separate if and else statements under the function. On the if function, I added that x is 
greater A and B is greater than x, it will return the density equation. If it is anything else, then in the else function,
I added that it will return zero. 

### b) 

Next lets improve our function to work correctly for a vector of x values. Modify your function in part (a) so that the core logic uses a for-loop statement and the loop moves through each element of x in succession. Since this is a bit more of a complex task, your function should look something like this: 

```{r}
duniform <- function(x, a, b){
  output <- NULL
  for( i in seq(x) ){ 
    if( x[i] >= a & x[i] <= b ) {  
      return(1/(b-a)) 
    }else{
    output[i] <- 0  
    }
  }
  return(output)
}


```

```{r}
data.frame( x=seq(-1, 12, by=.001) ) %>%
  mutate( y = duniform(x, 4, 8) ) %>%
  ggplot( aes(x=x, y=y) ) +
  geom_step()
```

For subquestion b, I put seq(x) after the i function to make it so that it will look at every element in x. Then, in the 
if line, I repeated what I put in subquestin a, except for adding in the [i]. In the else function, I added the output[i]. 

### c) 

 Install the R package microbenchmark. We will use this to discover the average duration (time) your function takes to execute code.
 
```{r, warning = FALSE}
microbenchmark::microbenchmark( duniform( seq(-4,12,by=.0001), 4, 8), times=100) 
```
 
### d) 

Instead of using a for loop, it might have been easier to use an ifelse() command, which inherently accepts vectors. Rewrite your function one last time, this time avoiding the for loop and instead introducing the vectorizable ifelse() command. Verify that your function works correctly by producing a plot of a uniform density. Finally, run the microbenchmark() code above again.

```{r}
duniform <- function(x, a, b) {
  ifelse(a <= x & b >= x, 1/(b-a), 0) 
}
 
data.frame( x=seq(-1, 12, by=.001) ) %>%
  mutate( y = duniform(x, 4, 8) ) %>%
  ggplot( aes(x=x, y=y) ) +
  geom_step()

microbenchmark::microbenchmark( duniform( seq(-4,12,by=.0001), 4, 8), times=100) 
```

For subquestion d, I created the ifelse function by first putting expressions of x >= a and x <= b using the & in the 
middle. Then, I added the equation in and the equation will work if the expressions are true. Finally, I added the 0 if the first two expressions were false. 

### e) 

Comment on Which version of your function was easier to write? Which ran faster?

I felt like the ifelse function was easier to write because it's all on one line and you say the conditions and expressions
right after. The ifelse function ran faster than the for loop function. 

