## Exercise 1

### a) 

This regular expression matches:

Any string that detects lower-case ab. 


```{r}
strings <- c('abs', 'Absorb', 'Above', 'abyss')
data.frame( string = strings ) %>%
  mutate( result = str_detect(string, 'ab') )
```

### b) 

This regular expression matches:

Any string that detects only lower-case letters a or b. 

```{r}
strings <- c('alchemist', 'bonfire', 'ABove', 'DAD')
data.frame( string = strings ) %>%
  mutate( result = str_detect(string, '[ab]') )
```

### c)

This regular expression matches:

Any string that ignores detecting lower-case letters a or b. 

```{r}
strings <- c('Fire', 'alchemist', 'beard', 'Arizona')
data.frame( string = strings ) %>%
  mutate( result = str_detect(string, '^[ab]') )
```

### d) 

This regular expression matches:

Any string that detects any repetition of one or more digits, any white space and anything with only letters a or A.

```{r}
strings <- c('aA200', 'bb300', 'A A 200', '300 a A', 'A2012 Absolve', '2012 Honda') 
data.frame( string = strings ) %>%
  mutate( result = str_detect(string, '\\d+\\s[aA]') )
```


### e) 

This regular expression matches:

Any string that has more than one digit, strings with zero or any white spaces, and any string that contains
letters a or A. 


```{r}
strings <- c('20 Absolve', 'The Honda is $30', 'Orange', 'Apple', '$3 Mandarins, $4 Apple Orange')
data.frame( string = strings ) %>%
  mutate( result = str_detect(string, '\\d+\\s*[aA]') )
```

### f)

This regular expression matches:

Any string that contains any character, with zero or any number of characters. 

```{r}
strings <- c('Food', 'The pizza is $20', '30', 'R', '')
data.frame( string = strings ) %>%
  mutate( result = str_detect(string, '.*') )
```

### g)

This regular expression matches:

Any string that detects, in the beginning input, any alphanumeric characters including upper case and lower case letters,
and numbers 0-9, with exactly 2 repetitions of those alphanumeric characters in the beginning of the input, and letters
that exactly match bar after the beginning input. 

```{r}
strings <- c('aabar', 'rabar', 'lbar', '2bar', '23bar', 'bar23')
data.frame( string = strings ) %>%
  mutate( result = str_detect(string, '^\\w{2}bar') )
```

### h)

This regular expression matches:

Any string that detects and captures the first group consisting of letters matching exactly foo, then exactly the period
character, then the letters matching exactly bar OR captures the second group which detects, at the beginning of the input,
any alphanumeric character or letter, with 2 repetitions of the alphanumeric needed, with the letters exactly matching bar
afterwards. 

```{r}
strings <- c('23bar', 'foo.bar', 'fooabar', 'foo1bar', '4bar', '4abar')
data.frame( string = strings ) %>%
  mutate( result = str_detect(string, '(foo\\.bar)|(^\\w{2}bar)') )
```

## Exercise 2

Produce a data frame with columns corresponding to the site, plot, camera, year, month, day, hour, minute, and second for these three file names. So we want to produce code that will create the data frame:

```{r}
file.names <- c( 'S123.P2.C10_20120621_213422.jpg',
                 'S10.P1.C1_20120622_050148.jpg',
                 'S187.P2.C2_20120702_023501.jpg')

file.names2 <- str_replace_all(file.names, pattern = '\\_', replacement = '\\.') 
file.names3 <- str_split_fixed(file.names2, pattern = '\\.', n = 6) 

Year <- str_sub(file.names3[,4], start = 1, end = 4) 
Month <- str_sub(file.names3[,4], start = 5, end = 6) 
